WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:12.080
The far right wing and Germany are two things
that sound pretty scary when you put them

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.080
together...

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.619
This is why, even though Angela Merkel has
won the elections with a high margin, it has

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:18.230
been a bittersweet victory.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.270
The fact that “Alternative for Germany”,
a xenophobic, ultra conservative party, has

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.779
become the third largest political force is
bad enough to make Merkel rather uneasy.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:31.579
“We don’t need to beat around the bush,
We wanted a better result, that is clear.”

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:32.579
–Ángela Merkel

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:35.720
Here at VisualPolitik, we had planned to start
this week talking about Germany…

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.969
but current news forced us to postpone that
so we would have time to prepare this video,

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:44.559
explaining what Alternative for Germany (or
AfD), is all about....

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:45.559
And…

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:48.300
yes, I know what you are thinking.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.510
“Come on Simon!

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.760
How could you not predict that the far right
would be a big hit in Germany?”

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.050
I mean, at the end of the day, xenophobic
parties in “fashion” all across Europe.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.309
Plus, AfD did great on the previous local
elections

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:12.070
Nonetheless, most of the polls said Germans
love moderation over all else.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.820
And that’s not just the electoral polls
but also sociological studies.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.280
For instance, while in France, only 50% of
the population places themselves on the center

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.090
of the spectrum, this number in Germany is
as high as 76%.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.870
Further, Germans have many reasons to be happy.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.320
They almost didn’t suffer at all during
the economic crisis and now their economy

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.960
is doing better than ever.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.590
Salaries are rising, there is almost full
employment, and companies export abroad as

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.399
if there were no tomorrow.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:40.380
So the big question here is…

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.040
Who votes for the far right wing in Germany?

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.870
And most important of all….

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:46.240
What does Alternative for Germany want?

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.450
Are they really a radical party or is the
media exaggerating?

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.789
Today we are going to answer to all of those
questions.

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:58.380
But before we get into that, let’s take
a look at the history!

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.760
A PARTY ON THE DIVIDE

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.670
Yes, it is true: today, Alternative for Germany
is a populist political party with a big focus

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.899
on rejecting immigrants and refugees.

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:11.460
Specially they want to reject those who come
from Islamic countries.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.980
But despite all of this, its origins couldn’t
be more different than what it is now.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.800
Let’s go back to the year 2013.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:24.730
Back then, all the European media talked about
the debt crisis in countries like Spain, Italy

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.480
and, especially, Greece.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.320
In case you don’t know the history here,
let’s have a quick summary.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.230
Before 2008, German banks invested a lot of
money in lending money to companies and governments

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.560
in the South of Europe.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.129
After the financial crisis of 2008, those
countries couldn’t give the money back.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:47.959
In fact, they needed even more cash so they
could afford their current spending: things

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:52.050
like the salaries of the civil servants, highways
and hospitals.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:56.550
As you can imagine, paying day to day spending
with debt it’s far from an ideal situation...

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:02.959
So Angela Merkel came along and said ‘OK,
guys…

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:04.330
I will lend you some money…

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:09.010
I will even let you give it back whenever
you can… but you’ve got to make some reforms

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.819
to your economic systems’.

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:13.409
This reform was AUSTERITY.

00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:18.080
Germany, along with other Northern European
countries, wanted to lend money to Italy,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:25.280
Spain and Greece while demanding budget cuts
so they would no longer need to borrow money

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.209
in the future… and they could afford to
pay their debts.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.840
Those conditions were seen as draconian by
most of these Southern European countries…

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.670
Who is Germany to tell us how we should spend
our money?

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.910
Meanwhile, some Germans were also mad at their
government…

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:43.190
“Who are you, Merkel, to go around giving
money to everyone?

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.239
This is the European Union!

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.250
Not an NGO!”.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:53.489
Among those angry Germans, there were three
famous economists: ALEXANDER GAULAND, BERN

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:55.459
LUCKE and KONRAD ADAM.

00:03:55.459 --> 00:04:00.409
These were the founders of the party ELECTORAL
ALTERNATIVE 2013, which would later become

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:01.799
Alternative for Germany.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:06.349
Many of the first supporters where bankers
and university teachers from the Western part

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:09.250
of Germany - cities like FRANKFURT, COLOGNE
or BOHN.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:17.579
Remember this, because it will become very
important later on.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:22.140
In their electoral manifesto, they suggested
Germany should leave the EURO and go back

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.730
to the German MARK.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:24.780
And why was that?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.780
Well… because this way they could avoid
bailouts to poorer economies.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:34.130
And, even more importantly, they could protect
their economy from a likely devaluation of

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.130
the EURO...

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:40.970
As we’ve said in other videos, Germany’s
success was, partly, due to their strong currency.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:48.400
But Spain and Greece was pushing the European
Central Bank to print more money so they could

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.919
pay their debts back.

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:53.419
And… of course, that sounded pretty bad
to many economists.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:59.440
So at first AFD voters had nothing to do with
your stereotype of a extreme right wing hooligan.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.509
They were businessmen, economists and university
lecturers.

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:06.910
This explains why the media started calling
this party the ‘teachers party’ and why

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:12.050
they couldn’t even win a single MP during
the federal elections of 2013.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:16.490
Think about it… monetary policy is not the
sexiest topic for an electoral campaign.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:24.750
Only our subscribers are interested in those
kind of topics!

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.889
Nonetheless, the fate of Alternative for Germany
was to change in July 2015.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:33.530
At that moment, the economist BERN LUCKE was
the chairman.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.919
But the brand new leader, FRAUKE PETRY had
other plans.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:41.160
Unlike the previous leaders, Petry came from
the Eastern part of the country.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.810
And this means she brought a totally different
political culture.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.570
For her, monetary policy, deficit and fiscal
responsibility was not so important.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:55.139
Instead, she knew there was a big niche of
voters who wanted an iron fist on things like

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:57.340
immigration and national identity.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.880
Besides, these kind of suggestions would give
her the support of other leaders like the

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.190
Nigel Farage, from Britain’s UKIP party,
Marine Le Pen, from the National Front in

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:10.010
France, and, of course, our favourite politician,
the VisualPolitik mascot… yes, my dear viewer,

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.940
of course we are talking about the one and
only…

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:20.270
“Leader of Germany’s far right party meets
Putin allies in Moscow”

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.849
I know, I know…

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:29.949
Vladimir Putin is an ever popular topic here
at VisualPolitik… but how can resist talking

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:30.949
about him!?

00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:35.660
Now, during this time, thousands of refugees
from Syria, Iraq and Libya came to Germany...

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.610
And Angela Merkel welcomed them with arms
wide open.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:40.610
And…

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.730
some Germans didn’t like this.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:48.310
So in the regional elections of 2016, Alternative
for Germany launched an electoral program

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:51.819
which had nothing to do with what the founders
had in mind at the beginning...

00:06:51.819 --> 00:06:55.389
This is why many of those economists jumped
ship...

00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:58.870
Among them, Bern Lucke, who was the first
president.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:06.039
“This party has fallen irretrievably into
the wrong hands” – Bern Lucke

00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:09.879
Since then, Alternative for Germany has had
two factions fighting each other.

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:12.819
On one hand we have the people leftover from
this first group.

00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:15.350
But they are less and less important everyday.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.130
On the other hand, we have those who came
under the leadership of Frauke Petry.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:24.080
This new political structure means more votes,
but also more problems:

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:30.310
The AfD’s current crisis was triggered in
mid-January through a taboo-breaking speech

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.169
by the rightwinger Björn Höcke, who in a
beer hall in Dresden called for a “180 degree

00:07:35.169 --> 00:07:38.930
turn” in Germany’s culture of remembering
and atoning for the Nazi era.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.220
Regardless of how much far right wing you
want to be, these kind of things are not really

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:43.979
cool in Germany.

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:46.389
And this created many problems to Mrs. Petry...

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:51.520
There are investigations open because, apparently,
some of the proposals on her program might

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:52.819
be illegal.

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:57.430
Remember that German law is made to avoid
Nazi backlash.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:02.620
The issues in this party are so large that
even Frauke Petry herself had to resign one

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:09.400
day after this last election due to fights
with some more extreme elements.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:14.980
Nevertheless, with or without the unfortunate
quotes and the internal division, Alternative

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:18.020
for Germany has become the big surprise from
this election.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.190
So the question now is…

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.220
Who voted for them?

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:27.030
Well, let’s take a look at this…

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.720
A HISTORY OF TWO GERMANIES

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.180
Over the past few years the German economy
has been on fire.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:37.090
Salaries are growing, companies are exporting
and inequality is almost nonexistent.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:43.300
Well… actually, hold on a minute, that last
bit is not sure...

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.820
Yes, of course, there is almost no poverty
on the streets, and there are factories all

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:47.820
over the country...

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:52.790
But when we look at the income distribution
by regions, we see a clear division.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:09:04.150
If you pay attention to this map, you will
see a blue part and a red part.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.890
The blue part is the wealthiest.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.720
The red is the poorest.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.770
The blue part is what used to be Western Germany.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:15.700
The red part is what it used to be Eastern
Germany - communist Germany.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:20.220
The Germany that went capitalist in 1989 but
still doesn’t have the same standard of

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.480
living as the West.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.500
If you visit a city like DRESDEN, in Eastern
Germany, you will see lots of construction

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:26.500
going on.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.880
Most of the buildings were completed just
a couple of years ago.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.160
The reunification of Germany is still very
much an ongoing process.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.500
Of course, if we compare the standard of life
of an Eastern German with a Greek or a Spaniard,

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.280
it doesn’t look that bad.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.290
But Germans like to be compared with other
Germans.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:10:07.850
And then things look way worse…

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:12.910
In this city, the city of SCHWEDT, Alternative
for Germany got over 20% of the votes.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:17.360
And a little further south, in the state of
Saxony, most people are voting for the far

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.360
right...

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.310
But hold on a minute because this not just
about the economy!

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:23.170
We are talking about a totally different culture
here.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.170
Think about it…

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:29.290
East Germans lived for entire generations
under a totally different political system.

00:10:29.290 --> 00:10:33.540
This means a different education, a different
lifestyle, and different values.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.420
Here, moderation is not good thing.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.190
In fact, the LEFT WING party, DIE LINKE, gets
most of its votes in Eastern Berlin.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:45.090
But there is more than that!

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:49.480
While Western Germans spent decades remembering
their Nazi past, the Government of the Democratic

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.350
Republic of Germany tried to create a new
sense of national pride.

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:57.110
So, when an AFD candidate says that we must
appreciate the courage of the German soldiers

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:01.850
during World War II, Germans from Cologne
are frightened but Germans from Leipzig say

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:02.970
‘OK, whatever!’.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.320
Okay, but now there is an important question...

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.210
Why is Eastern Germany so much against refugees
and immigrants?

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:13.700
Well… many of you might think this is because
they are tired of Muslims who don’t want

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.630
to integrate on their culture, right?

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:19.790
Well… here it comes a number that is going
to blow your mind.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:28.300
There are 9 million immigrants in Germany…
and less than 500.000 live in Eastern Germany.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:32.510
It makes total sense.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:35.730
Those who move to Germany want to be in the
wealthiest regions.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:39.520
So if you walk down the streets of Dresden,
you will hardly see a woman covering her head

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:40.520
with a HIJAB.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:43.880
And guess who is the biggest immigrant community
in Eastern Germany!?

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:44.880
Surprise, surprise!

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.310
It’s THE RUSSIANS!

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.640
More than half of the immigrants living on
this side of the country come from the land

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:50.640
of Putin.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.560
And, of course, they are the biggest audience
for German Russia Today.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:57.430
And this explains things like this…

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:02.290
AfD deploys double-headed eagle to snare Russian-German
voters

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:03.660
So now the question is…

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:08.060
Do you think Alternative for Germany can keep
growing in the next elections or will internal

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.430
divisions will end up destroying the party?

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:13.070
What do you think they can do during the following
four years?

